{"title":"What I’ve been reading","link":"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/967366946/0/marginalrevolution~What-Ive-been-reading.html","date":1786164197000,"content":"<p>1. Mike Jay, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Wireheads-Unnatural-History-Technology-Brain/dp/0300278888/ref=sr_1_1?crid=VNGHOFP6CBFC&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.CFsSkYgyeViAO-UC4hLfKA.ejFFxKYEboNziXDTCXM_1FUNMPm3aqVs-FUzsD6cI18&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=Wireheads%3A+An+Unnatural+History+of+Technology+in+the+Brain&amp;nsdOptOutParam=true&amp;qid=1785914144&amp;sprefix=wireheads+an+unnatural+history+of+technology+in+the+brain%2Caps%2C463&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">Wireheads: An Unnatural History of Technology in the Brain</a>.  Much of this story is told through the lens of the accompanying science fiction and non-neuro trends in other parts of science.  An excellent book, substantive on every page, and it will make you want to read some of the works he cites.</p>\r\n<p>2. Vasily Grossman, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Front-Line-Stalingrad-Treblinka-Berlin-1941-45/dp/B0F7G427YD/ref=sr_1_1?crid=38BFQPJ8QLS04&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.ziDEGGOBbjrY4x_Nz8bFYuReInrCbqo6sTzSfg9Y6tCuOUxc1WkJ7a6Gh7VcJkqgMBi4CsBV-4tfZw9fCb9yRcpYxIN7cd0Mrb8Z6FzlKtw.nwAdzvUjDb-L5W_geYqHJ2HZs8czpIIjtdV5_13AyOA&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=grossman+from+the+front+line&amp;qid=1785914104&amp;sprefix=grossman+from+the+f%2Caps%2C309&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">From the Front Line, Stalingrad-Treblinka-Berlin 1941-1945</a>.  Self-recommending, the best third of this volume is just stunningly good.  You do not have to read the whole thing, as there is some degree of repetition.</p>\r\n<p>3. Forough Farrokhzad, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Let-Believe-Beginning-Cold-Season/dp/0811231658/ref=sr_1_1?crid=2K1A1CI0SQYJQ&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.uli3jNJBsqHH7ubQoVeRezhy4rlBvKVZ1aRiBdvXB8rxA-hsTZbFQ5A2tQLg5bma.XK8tyuh0RCG0gfUpDPX8MyTjM5Ll4_DUr8M-vMW1cHU&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=Let+Us+Believe+in+the+Beginning+of+the+Cold+Season&amp;nsdOptOutParam=true&amp;qid=1785914032&amp;sprefix=let+us+believe+in+the+beginning+of+the+cold+season%2Caps%2C287&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">Let Us Believe in the Beginning of the Cold Season</a>.  She died in a car crash in 1967 at the age of thirty-three, but it turns out she was an amazing poet.  I usually do not enjoy poetry in translation, but this is so fiery and full of passion that I am happy to recommend it.  Surely in the original Farsi it is better (and quite different?) yet.</p>\r\n<p>4. André Breton, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Nadja-Andr%C3%A9-Breton/dp/1681379368/ref=sr_1_1?crid=3O20SW79W07AC&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.c4QswUrVZ9wIL53O74869GH7871up7I1dcQ_Nt5zDXvJk2o_8Ukrpzkxv-NMz8yH9dZM_zDuT_EPV8yUK1YtXzlf2t7PCJ1occDP2_RAzwkZEzW7_Ggr-4-necgY0OGd6z55t1MYDlU81vYHIuMD8cms_th4hUE-mw2qszDVUOdwJ-RdRSdXJgPaYfKERKR_zMICBD_HksAMkicKaaM0H0plRAhI63lQ7UEHx0toxRk.VGsCRu8bg_e2gb5e_jix7yJRjMMvwpc5tdhZeObibzc&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=nadja&amp;qid=1785913995&amp;sprefix=https+%2F%2Fwww.amazon.com%2Fnot-built-day-slavery-empire%2Fdp%2F1668089556%2Fref+sr_1_1+crid+2aptfztrp1hf5%26dib+eyj2ijo%2Caps%2C3564&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">Nadja</a>.  It is short, and fun enough to read.  But one learns most books from the past are not masterpieces, and that surrealism as a movement — most of all in painting — was more important than the literary outputs of surrealism.  Nadja, to me, sounds like a drag.  Which is fine.  But.  What does Breton really think he is immortalizing here?  At the end he writes “Beauty will be CONVULSIVE or not at all.”  I do appreciate Breton for his understanding of Mexico, and for <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Andr%C3%A9_Breton\" target=\"_blank\">his cultivation of Haitian art</a>.</p>\r\n<p>5. Emma Southon, <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://www.amazon.com/Not-Built-Day-Slavery-Empire/dp/1668089556/ref=sr_1_1?crid=2APTFZTRP1HF5&amp;dib=eyJ2IjoiMSJ9.RFLhAQ90BLFmWaVxgXPDBowg-0q5n-JSn0Ua5eHak2jGjHj071QN20LucGBJIEps.orgXV6INt_VyJewadSU3BEHyLzl9VNa_jBi76qPVtoI&amp;dib_tag=se&amp;keywords=Servus%3A+How+Slavery+Made+the+Roman+Empire&amp;qid=1785913941&amp;sprefix=servus+how+slavery+made+the+roman+empire%2Caps%2C266&amp;sr=8-1\" target=\"_blank\">Servus: How Slavery Made the Roman Empire</a>.  A very good and aggressively readable book about how terrible slavery was in the Roman Empire.  A useful corrective to other sources as well.  While my general view is that interpretative disputes over antiquity are difficult to resolve, I find her interpretations more intuitive and more convincing than the other side of the debate.</p>\r\n<p>The post <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com/marginalrevolution/2026/08/what-ive-been-reading-293.html\">What I’ve been reading</a> appeared first on <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/t/0/0/marginalrevolution/~https://marginalrevolution.com\">Marginal REVOLUTION</a>.</p>\r\n<img height=\"1\" width=\"1\" src=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/~/i/967366946/0/marginalrevolution\" />\r\n<div><a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/28/967366946/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/fblike20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/29/967366946/marginalrevolution,\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/pinterest20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/24/967366946/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/x.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.feedblitz.com/_/19/967366946/marginalrevolution\"><img height=\"20\" src=\"https://assets.feedblitz.com/i/email20.png\" /></a> <a href=\"https://feeds.f